From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 07:59:31 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 149/160] feat: the grade travels with the capture - an ASC
 .cdl sidecar and an honest rec/cdl (S10 item 3a)

A captured EXR now arrives in Resolve with the grade that made it, as a file
Resolve can actually apply rather than a line a human has to retype.

The sidecar is an ASC ColorDecisionList - urn:ASC:CDL:v1.01, one ColorDecision
holding one ColorCorrection with SOPNode (Slope/Offset/Power) and SatNode
(Saturation), six fixed decimals, element order as the ASC schema and
cdl_convert write it. Nothing about the format is invented: the file has to open
in an application this tree cannot test against. Resolve's ColorTrace lists .cdl
and .ccc XML (and a CMX EDL carrying SOP in its comments) as the importable
formats, and an individual file is brought in through Gallery -> Stills ->
right-click -> Import and applied by hand, which is the right shape for a
single-camera engine take.

One file per TAKE, not per frame: <prefix>.cdl beside a sequence with the prefix
as the ColorCorrection id, <prefix>.%06d.cdl beside a single frame (each single
frame is its own take and already has its own number). It is claimed by the
first frame of a capture and written when that frame's readback comes home - the
first moment the numbers are known to belong to a file that exists - on the
render thread, a few hundred bytes once per capture, because the writer job owns
pixels and nothing else and the sidecar must sit beside the FIRST file rather
than beside whichever one a job thread finished first. Naming follows the
existing r_SceneReferredExportSequenceNaming (46f9e8e4) for free, since it is
built from the same latched folder and prefix.

A neutral grade is still written: a missing file cannot be told apart from a
writer that failed, and an identity CDL is a positive statement.
r_SceneReferredExportCDL 0 opts out.

One CDL cannot describe an animated grade, and every grade property is now
TrackView-animatable. Rather than silently standing by frame zero's numbers or
spraying a file per frame, later frames are compared against what was written
and ONE warning names the first frame at which the sidecar stopped being the
whole truth. Each frame's own rec/cdl is still correct for that frame.

Two defects in the existing metadata are fixed on the way, both found by reading
it against ToneMapping.cpp:628-672:

  * GatherMetadata() never read Global_User_GradeBypass, so a frame captured
    with Bypass Grade ticked recorded a CDL that was demonstrably not in the
    picture. A bypassed frame now carries the identity, marked "(bypassed)".
  * it did not apply the renderer's 1e-4 floors on slope and power, so a header
    could describe a frame that was not rendered.

rec/cdl also moves from %g to the ASC's six fixed decimals, so the EXR header and
the .cdl beside it can be compared by eye. The metadata struct now carries the
CDL as numbers as well as text, which is what the sidecar writes from - the
string is a readout, not a data path.

Nothing about what an EXR CONTAINS has changed: the tap, the encoding, the
attributes' meaning and the ring are untouched. Which tap you hold decides what
the sidecar is for - beside a graded tap it is provenance (what was baked in),
beside the negative it is the starting point (apply it and the day's grade is
back). docs/SceneReferredExport.md gains section 4.1 for the format and 5.5 for
the round trip that is this item's acceptance, including which camera operators
have to be neutral for the comparison to be exact and what each shape of
difference means.

Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s10-grade-component.md section 3a.5.
Research: scene-notes/research/s10-3a-wheels-cdl-trackview.md sections 1.3 and 5.

